1. Creating a Class
class Fruit {
    // properties and methods
}
class Fruit creates a class named Fruit.
A class is like a blueprint for creating objects.
2. Property
public $name = "Fruit name";
$name is a property of the Fruit class.
public means the property can be accessed from outside the class.
3. $this
return $this->name;
$this refers to the current object.
$this->name means the name property belonging to the current object.

For example:

$mango->name

Here, $this->name refers to $mango's name.

4. Method
public function getName(){
    return $this->name;
}
getName() is a method (function inside a class).
It returns the current value of $name.
5. Setting a Property Using a Method
public function setCarName($car_name){
    return $this->name = $car_name;
}
setCarName() changes the value of $name.
$car_name is the value passed to the method.
6. Creating an Object
$mango = new Fruit();
new Fruit() creates an object from the Fruit class.
$mango stores that object.
7. Changing a Property Directly
$mango->name = "BMW car";

This changes the name property of the $mango object.

8. Changing the Property Using a Method
$mango->setCarName("Nissan car");

Now the value of $name becomes:

Nissan car

The previous "BMW car" value is replaced.

9. Getting the Value
echo $mango->getName();

getName() returns the current value of $name, so the output is:

Nissan car
Important Concept

The basic flow is:

Class → Object → Property/Method

Fruit (Class)
   ↓
$mango (Object)
   ↓
$name (Property)
   ↓
getName() / setCarName() (Methods)
